WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.620 --> 00:00:05.570
Like most teas, oolong is made from the
leaves of a flowering plant species called

00:00:05.570 --> 00:00:07.190
Camellia Sinensis.

00:00:07.550 --> 00:00:12.980
Harvest time is during the plant's peak
growing season, which in Thailand is from May

00:00:12.980 --> 00:00:21.240
to November. Workers handpick what's known
as the flush, a grouping of two young leaves

00:00:21.240 --> 00:00:23.670
and a bud which grows out the top of the
plant.

00:00:25.080 --> 00:00:30.750
At this time of year, the plant produces a
new flush every 7 to 15 days.

00:00:34.570 --> 00:00:39.160
An experienced tea master directs every
phase of the processing.

00:00:39.190 --> 00:00:41.920
The first step of which is called solar
withering.

00:00:42.790 --> 00:00:47.890
Workers bring the leaves into a glass roofed
building, then spread them out in the sun for

00:00:47.890 --> 00:00:49.420
15 to 20 minutes.

00:00:49.720 --> 00:00:52.600
This kick starts the oxidation fermentation
process.

00:00:53.440 --> 00:00:59.230
As the chlorophyll enzymes inside the
wilting leaves start breaking down at the

00:00:59.230 --> 00:01:01.810
same time, the moisture inside begins
evaporating.

00:01:03.820 --> 00:01:08.620
Workers keep moving the leaves around to
ensure a thorough exposure to the sun.

00:01:08.650 --> 00:01:11.410
Then they gather up the leaves for step two.

00:01:11.440 --> 00:01:12.850
Indoor withering.

00:01:12.940 --> 00:01:19.620
The leaves lie on bamboo trays for 6 to 8
hours, where gently stirred every two hours,

00:01:19.630 --> 00:01:21.310
they oxidize further.

00:01:22.810 --> 00:01:24.690
Step three Disruption.

00:01:24.700 --> 00:01:27.220
The leaves go into a rotating drum.

00:01:27.460 --> 00:01:30.310
As they tumble, they bruise and tear.

00:01:30.430 --> 00:01:35.710
This breaks down the cell structures,
enabling oxygen to penetrate deep inside,

00:01:35.710 --> 00:01:38.110
greatly accelerating fermentation.

00:01:38.470 --> 00:01:43.630
This also releases the leaf juices, which
helps dry out the taste of the tea.

00:01:46.580 --> 00:01:51.620
When the tea master determines the leaves
have sufficiently oxidized, they stop the

00:01:51.620 --> 00:01:57.080
oxidation process by tumbling the leaves in
a gas heated dryer for 10 to 15 minutes.

00:01:58.150 --> 00:02:03.940
This fourth step of the process is called
fixation because it fixes the oxidation at

00:02:03.940 --> 00:02:10.000
the desired level, which can be anywhere
from 8 to 85%, depending on the variety of

00:02:10.000 --> 00:02:11.260
Oolong in production.

00:02:12.270 --> 00:02:16.890
This is the most critical part of the
process because it determines the tea's

00:02:16.890 --> 00:02:19.170
taste, aroma and color.

00:02:19.320 --> 00:02:22.590
The next step forms the tea leaves into tiny
pellets.

00:02:23.910 --> 00:02:28.920
First, workers shake the leaves on a sieve
to filter out the dust like particles.

00:02:29.100 --> 00:02:34.550
Then they bag the leaves in a cotton cloth
and place them first in a kneading machine.

00:02:34.560 --> 00:02:42.540
Then afterward in a rolling press, kneading
and rolling the bag, twist the leaves inside

00:02:42.540 --> 00:02:43.950
into tiny pellets.

00:02:44.490 --> 00:02:48.480
Forming these pellet shapes intensifies the
flavor of the tea.

00:02:48.490 --> 00:02:53.220
And when the tea is steeped in hot water,
releases that flavor slowly.

00:02:58.240 --> 00:03:03.670
They repeat the seething, kneading and
rolling cycle up to 35 times until the Tea

00:03:03.670 --> 00:03:05.950
Master is satisfied with the result.

00:03:11.080 --> 00:03:14.830
Then and only then does the final step begin
firing.

00:03:16.990 --> 00:03:22.240
They transfer the tea to an oven in which it
undergoes three drying cycles of 20 minutes

00:03:22.240 --> 00:03:24.430
each at a temperature of approximately
100°C.

00:03:29.120 --> 00:03:34.400
This dries the damp tea, reducing the
moisture content to the target level of less

00:03:34.400 --> 00:03:35.600
than 5%.

00:03:35.780 --> 00:03:38.540
The firing also brings out the fragrance.

00:03:40.040 --> 00:03:45.530
The traditional way to brew oolong is in a
clay teapot using about two teaspoons of tea

00:03:45.560 --> 00:03:50.180
per cup. Ideally, the water should be 90 to
100°C.

00:03:51.230 --> 00:03:56.690
Steeping time is from 3 to 10 minutes and
you can brew the same leaves up to five

00:03:56.690 --> 00:03:57.410
times.

